# patch: 003-interactive-inject-chunked
# format: CHUNKED ONLY (2.1.242+). Monolithic keeps 003-interactive-inject.patch.
# version: authored against 2.1.260
# depends: 002
# description: Make remote_input work in the interactive Ink TTY REPL.
#              The sidecar (002) injects via StructuredIO.prependUserMessage,
#              which only the stream-json input loop drains — so injected prompts
#              never execute in interactive mode. This registers
#              globalThis.__claudiverseSubmit pointing at the live submit path.
#
# RE-ANCHORED ONTO A BETTER LANDMARK THAN THE MONOLITHIC PATCH HAD.
#   The 2.1.238 hunk hung off `Sut = React_REPL.useRef(vse); Sut.current = vse;`
#   inside the REPL component, and its own header flagged `Sut` as a minified
#   name on an ADDED line — the dangerous case. That shape is GONE on chunked:
#   the REPL component body is now React-Compiler output, a long run of
#   `if (Vi[413] !== Uu.submit)` memo-cache slots where every local is an
#   unanchorable minified name in a numbered slot. Re-contexting was not an
#   option, so the hook was moved UPSTREAM to the owner of the submit path.
#
#   New site: class `PromptSubmitController`.bindHost(), which is the
#   host-binding step the REPL performs on mount — the same lifecycle moment the
#   old useEffect used, one layer closer to the submitter.
#
# ANCHOR QUALITY (better than the patch it replaces):
#   The hunk's context contains the STRING LITERAL
#     'PromptSubmitController used before its host was bound'
#   which is unique across the whole tree and identical on 2.1.259 AND 2.1.260
#   (measured on both). A string literal cannot be renamed by a minifier, so
#   this hunk is anchored on something that does not drift with the bundler.
#
# 🔴 ONE MINIFIED NAME ON AN ADDED LINE: `Tb`.
#   `Tb` is the shared no-op input-controls object
#     var Tb = { setCursorOffset: () => {}, clearBuffer: () => {}, resetHistory: () => {} };
#   i.e. exactly the literal the monolithic patch inlined by hand. It is used
#   because this file ALREADY calls `this.submit(w, Tb)` in submitSurveyFollowUp,
#   so the added line reproduces an existing in-tree call shape rather than
#   inventing one. Mitigation is honest but PARTIAL: `this.submit` and the class
#   are pinned by the string-literal context above, so a wrong binding cannot be
#   a wrong SUBMITTER — but a future reassignment of `Tb` would silently pass the
#   wrong options object. Inlining the literal instead would remove the last
#   minified name; it is left as-is to match the file's own idiom, and flagged
#   here rather than hidden.
#
# ============================================================================
# RE-AUTHORED 2026-09-07 — ANCHOR-DRIVEN, AND DELIBERATELY MADE LOUDER
# ============================================================================
# STATUS: 🔴 VERSION-LOCKED BY CHOICE. Applies to 2.1.263 (verified); FAILS on
#         2.1.260 (verified). An EARLIER draft of this patch applied cleanly to
#         BOTH trees — and that version was WRONG. See below; this is the one
#         finding in this batch that a green check would have hidden.
#
# THE TRAP THAT WAS CAUGHT:
#   The injected line passes an editor-helpers stub as submit()'s 2nd arg.
#   On 2.1.263 that stub is `$k`; on 2.1.260 the SAME object is named `Tb`.
#   A context-only anchoring (unique string 'PromptSubmitController used
#   before its host was bound', identical in both trees) made the hunk APPLY
#   to both. But `git apply` matches CONTEXT only — never added lines — so on
#   .260 it would have emitted a reference to `$k`, and on .260 `$k` IS A
#   DIFFERENT, LIVE LOCAL (`$k = Fc()`, a message queue, declared ~line 85128
#   inside another function and NOT in scope at the injection site ~75513).
#   Result: a clean `git apply --check` PASS that silently produces a
#   ReferenceError, swallowed by the try/catch — the feature dies quietly.
#   This is exactly the "applies but binds something entirely different"
#   failure the anchor work exists to prevent.
#
# THE FIX — bind the name where it is DEFINED, not where it is used:
#   Hunk 1 aliases the stub at its definition site:
#       globalThis.__cvSubmitHelpers = $k;
#   Hunk 2's added line then references ONLY `globalThis.__cvSubmitHelpers`.
#   No minified name appears on ANY added line in this patch.
#   Because hunk 1's context necessarily contains `var $k = {...}`, the patch
#   now FAILS LOUDLY on a tree where the stub is renamed, instead of applying
#   and misbinding. That is the intended trade: a loud, correct failure over a
#   green check that ships a dead feature.
#
# ANCHORS USED: 'PromptSubmitController used before its host was bound'
#   (UNIQUE, 1 occurrence, byte-identical in both trees — `bindHost` alone is
#   NOT usable: 7 occurrences in each tree) and the stub's own body literal
#   `{ setCursorOffset: () => {}, clearBuffer: () => {}, resetHistory: () => {} }`.
#
# ⛔ WHAT WOULD UNBLOCK IT: an anchor rule naming the editor-helpers stub.
#   Then hunk 1 anchors on a stable name and the whole patch goes
#   version-independent with no loss of loudness.
#
diff --git a/components/PromptInput/useSwarmBanner.js b/components/PromptInput/useSwarmBanner.js
index de8c6aa..978385d 100644
--- a/components/PromptInput/useSwarmBanner.js
+++ b/components/PromptInput/useSwarmBanner.js
@@ -16339,4 +16339,5 @@ F();
 F();
 var $k = { setCursorOffset: () => {}, clearBuffer: () => {}, resetHistory: () => {} };
+globalThis.__cvSubmitHelpers = $k;
 function V7(kpn) {
     let Qbe = _(8),
@@ -77084,10 +77089,14 @@ class Qce {
     constructor(w) {
         this.#e = w;
     }
     bindHost(w) {
         this.#t = w;
+        try {
+            globalThis.__claudiverseSubmit = (__text) =>
+                this.submit(__text, globalThis.__cvSubmitHelpers);
+        } catch (__e) {}
     }
     #o() {
         if (!this.#t) throw Error('PromptSubmitController used before its host was bound');
         return this.#t;
     }
